Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever an order is returned for a refund, the customer keeps the loyalty points earned on that order; the points are never taken back.
  2. Whenever a customer asks for a refund for a change of mind, refund only the item price (after any discount code) and keep the shipping fee. Refund 100% of the item price if the request is made up to 14 days after delivery. Refund 50% of the item price if it is made from day 15 through day 30 after delivery. Refund nothing after day 30. For example, for a 2,000 rupee shirt with 150 rupees shipping and no discount, a change-of-mind request within 14 days gets 2,000 rupees.
  3. Whenever a customer asks for a refund for damaged goods, refund the full amount including shipping if the request is made up to 90 days after delivery. For example, for a 2,000 rupee shirt with 150 rupees shipping and no discount, a damaged-goods request within 14 days gets 2,150 rupees.
  4. Whenever a refund is approved, return the money the way it was paid, whatever the reason for the refund. An order paid with store credit is refunded as store credit, and an order paid by card is refunded to that card.
  5. Whenever a rupee amount, including a refund, has a fractional part, round it down to the whole rupee. For example, a refund of 833.50 becomes 833.
  6. Whenever loyalty points are awarded, give points only for each full 100 rupees paid and round any fractional remainder down. For example, 12.9 points' worth of spend yields 12 points.
  7. Whenever a sales summary or daily summary shows "Orders", count every order in the list, including cancelled, refunded, and store-credit orders. It shows 0 only if there were no orders at all.
  8. Whenever a sales summary or daily summary shows "Revenue", add up the totals (items after discount plus shipping) of card orders only. Do not subtract refunds, and store-credit orders contribute nothing. If there are no card orders, revenue is 0.
  9. Whenever the daily summary shows the average order value, divide revenue by the number of card orders and round down to whole rupees. If there are no card orders, show 0, not blank and not an error.
